            <ArticleTitle>[CT-guided thoracal sympathicolysis for the treatment of peripheral arterial occlusive disease and chronic thoracal pain syndromes in 6 patients].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>920-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">CT-guided thoracal sympathicolysis for the treatment of peripheral arterial occlusive disease and chronic thoracal pain syndromes in 6 patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Retrospective evaluation of the safety and effectivity of CT-guided percutaneous thoracal sympathicolysis (CT-TSL) in the treatment of patients with peripheral arterial occlusive disease (PAOD) of the upper limb and chronic thoracal pain syndromes. Comparison of our own experience with literature reports.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Between 6/96 and 12/99, 4 patients with PAOD of the upper limb and two with chronic thoracal pain syndromes caused by herpes zoster were treated by unilateral CT-TSL.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">18, 21 and 32 months after the intervention 3 out of 4 patients treated for PAOD reported subjective improvements, and one remained unchanged. Two patients treated for pain syndromes showed no long-term benefit of the procedure. There were no serious complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The CT-TSL is an alternative method in the treatment of PAOD in patients who are unsuitable for treatment by revascularization.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
